src/RESPLE.cpp -- the RESPLE node itself.

One patch per upstream file, so this one patch carries every change the bridge
makes to RESPLE.cpp regardless of concern. To keep that from hiding the "why",
each hunk explains itself *in the source*, tagged `resple_bridge:` -- after
patching, `grep -n 'resple_bridge:' src/RESPLE.cpp` enumerates the entire local
delta with its rationale, in the staged file a reader is actually looking at.
This header is only an index; the reasoning has one home, at the sites.

1. Visibility. `private:` -> `public:` so cpp/bindings.cpp can inject sensor
   data into RESPLE's own buffers and read spline state. No behavior change.
2. Finish/synchronization condition. processData()'s `while (true)` gets a
   state-based exit and publishes stable-blocked submission epochs while a
   bridge-owned mutex makes each worker pass an explicit synchronization unit.
   After publication the worker releases that mutex and honors an explicit
   handoff request before it may start another pass, preventing lock starvation.
3. Queue discipline and tickets. Pop under the queues' own mutex; notify the
   bridge on every dequeue, and carry a sweep ticket through raw, downsampled,
   discarded, initialization, and estimator-batch queues using lockstep
   sidecars, checked with resple_bridge::check_invariant rather than assert
   (the shipped build is Release, so assert would compile the checks out
   exactly where a desynced sidecar becomes undefined behavior). Upstream
   PointData and estimator APIs stay unchanged.
4. Deterministic initialization(): LIO waits for the fixed 15-sample gravity
   prefix, the initial map is built only from the *complete* fixed 100 ms
   window, and propRCP(start_t_ns) moves below both retry gates so it runs
   exactly once. These prevent initialization state from depending on how far
   the producer ran before the worker. Single-LiDAR replay with sufficient IMU
   lead never entered the retry path, so its trajectory is bit-identical.
5. An actionable failure when a complete 100 ms window holds fewer than the
   required 100 downsampled points; more input cannot change a closed window.
6. Safe pre-initialization lifecycle. `spline` starts as nullptr rather than an
   indeterminate pointer, so a starved session can finish and report no pose
   instead of dereferencing garbage before initFilter assigns the real spline.
7. An upstream out-of-bounds read: processData() takes pt_meas.back() on every
   true return from collectMeasurements(), which also covers a pass that
   discarded every point it popped and left pt_meas empty.
8. Measurement composition deliberately remains upstream's. Offline
   determinism comes from timestamp-ordered producer submission, atomic sweep
   enqueue/dequeue, and explicit stable ticket boundaries. Additional IMU or
   LiDAR extension gates change nonlinear filter batch composition throughout
   a run; they do not merely remove a scheduling race. The worker's explicit
   close/no-progress lifecycle then applies upstream's finite-buffer readiness
   at end-of-stream without a custom gate stranding processable input.

Hunks are generated with `diff -U3`, never hand-trimmed, except concern 8's
single-condition replacement: its adjacent upstream closing lines contain
trailing whitespace, so that hunk retains the two clean following context
lines instead of copying whitespace into this patch. What `-F0` requires is an
*exact* context match, not three lines of it, so the shortened hunk still
applies at zero fuzz and zero offset; fewer context lines only narrow the
evidence of drift, which is why this stays the single exception.

